<section class="panel" id="tab-shortlist" data-name="shortlist">
{{ plain('shortlist') }}
<h2>Candidates for experimental validation {{ q('shortlist') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">The {{ SL.rows | length }} first-in-class genes of the Discovery tab, each checked against the questions a laboratory would ask before testing it, including what the UC and IBD papers on it say. A light per criterion: green meets it, amber partly, red fails it, grey not measured. Hover over a light for the values. Points are green 2, amber 1, grey 0, red −1 (a null genetic test scores 0; the mouse column is not scored). Many genes tie on points, so the table gives <strong>groups, not positions</strong>: how stable each gene is under six ways of scoring (see below). Every value comes from the other tabs; nothing here is a new estimate.</p>
<div class="sl-legend"><span><i class="lt green"></i> meets</span><span><i class="lt amber"></i> partly</span><span><i class="lt red"></i> fails</span><span><i class="lt grey"></i> not measured</span></div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>The criteria</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Reproducible:</strong> significant increase in at least 6 of 8 cohorts (amber 4–5), and the change holds after adjusting for colon segment, age and sex.</li>
<li><strong>Protein:</strong> the protein also rises in the independent proteomics study. Grey when mass spectrometry did not quantify it. This light favours abundant genes (see the box on groups).</li>
<li><strong>Tissue change:</strong> more RNA per cell, or just more of the cells that make it? Two views: the bulk composition adjustment (lineage level) and the change inside the gene's own single-cell clusters, inflamed against healthy, which also sees shifts within a lineage such as crypt expansion. Green: the per-cell rise is at least 2-fold (3-fold when the bulk verdict is only partial); red: no per-cell rise (under 0.3 log2), so the single cells explain the increase by cell numbers. Checked on markers: DUOX2, MMP3 and CHI3L1 rise 16- to 140-fold per cell; TNF, IL1B and OSM do not, as expected for genes brought in by immune cells.</li>
<li><strong>Cell source:</strong> at least half of the gene's RNA comes from one cell family in inflamed single cells (amber 30–50 %), and that family is also the main source in at least two of the three other single-cell atlases (Garrido-Trigo 2023, Gubatan 2026 and the anti-TNF atlas GSE282122; otherwise amber): you know which cell to test. Also amber: lining genes concentrated in the crypt zone (≥ 76 % of their epithelial RNA in stem, transit-amplifying and progenitor cells, halfway between EPCAM and the crypt markers LGR5 and OLFM4) that rise there. These may belong to the repair programme of the crypt, and blocking them could slow healing. The same check for the stroma: fibroblast genes with ≥ {{ (SL.summary.niche_threshold * 100) | round | int }} % of their fibroblast RNA in the crypt-niche fibroblasts (WNT2B+ and RSPO3+, which feed the stem cells; not the inflammatory fibroblasts) that rise per cell there. The threshold is halfway between a generic fibroblast gene (COL1A2 {{ (SL.summary.niche_markers.generic.COL1A2 * 100) | round | int }} %) and the niche markers WNT2B, GREM1 and RSPO3 ({{ (SL.summary.niche_markers.niche.WNT2B * 100) | round | int }}–{{ (SL.summary.niche_markers.niche.RSPO3 * 100) | round | int }} %). {% if SL.summary.niche_flag %}Flagged: {{ SL.summary.niche_flag | join(", ") }}.{% else %}No candidate is flagged: the stromal genes of group A have {% for g, v in SL.summary.niche_stromal.items() if v.group == "A" %}{{ g }} {{ (v.niche_share * 100) | round | int }} %{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} of their fibroblast RNA in the niche, the level of a generic fibroblast gene.{% endif %}</li>
<li><strong>Genes made by neutrophils:</strong> when neutrophils make more than half of a gene's RNA in inflamed mucosa (Gubatan 2026, the only atlas that captures them), its cell family reads "neutrophil": {{ SL.summary.neutrophil_directed | join(", ") }}. These are neutrophil-directed candidates, like CXCR2 among the clinical targets: blocking them would act on the arrival and activity of neutrophils, which are found almost only in inflamed mucosa, rather than on a change of the tissue. That is a legitimate strategy, with two caveats: neutrophils also defend the damaged mucosa and help it heal, and in this atlas the neutrophil genes track severity in placebo arms too. Their tissue light is measured inside the neutrophils of Gubatan 2026 (inflamed against uninflamed UC mucosa, since that atlas has no healthy colon), with the same thresholds as for the other genes: S100A8 and CXCR2 rise per neutrophil, while ADGRE3 does not (its rise is all neutrophil arrival, so its light is red). Their cell-source light is amber, because the source is known from one atlas only. The first experiment is in blood neutrophils. Cell family also reads "endothelial" when vessels hold most of a gene's RNA (the lineage grouping folds vessels into stroma).</li>
<li><strong>Causal genetics:</strong> genetically higher level raises UC risk (green, supports inhibition) or lowers it (red); amber when tested without effect (it scores 0: a null test is not support); grey when no genetic instrument exists, which is the case for almost all new genes.</li>
<li><strong>Druggable:</strong> green with an antibody route (UniProt topology: an extracellular domain, a GPI anchor or secretion; a "membrane" location alone is not enough, because many proteins sit on its inner face) or strong small-molecule evidence in Open Targets (a high-quality ligand or pocket, a druggable family, a clinical drug). Amber with a pocket score ≥ 0.6 alone, a weak bucket, or a protein secreted but mainly in the ER. The pocket score alone ranked the UC small-molecule targets 4th to 25th of 45 proteins, so it does not decide.</li>
<li><strong>Safety:</strong> four flags: expression above 50 nTPM in vital organs, loss-of-function constraint, known safety events, and a Mendelian disease caused by loss of the gene (UniProt, susceptibility loci excluded). Green with none, amber with one, red with two or more.</li>
<li><strong>UC literature:</strong> every paper that names the gene with ulcerative colitis (or with colitis, IBD or Crohn's disease in the title or abstract) was read by a classifier agent: what kind of evidence (only in a list, expression, biomarker, genetic, functional) and, for functional studies, which direction. Each call is backed by a sentence copied from the paper and checked automatically. Green: a perturbation study shows a harmful role, so blocking it should help, and none shows the opposite. Red: perturbation studies show a protective role only, so blocking it may harm. Amber: a specific result on expression, a biomarker or genetics, or studies in both directions. Grey: no paper, or the gene is only named in a list of many genes or in a review. Only papers in a colitis or IBD setting count: of the papers that name these genes with UC anywhere in the text, most do so in a supplementary table or a list, or are about another disease. Functional results in other inflammatory settings are shown as context. The full list, gene by gene, is under "What the papers say" below.</li>
<li><strong>Mouse:</strong> whether the gene was ever perturbed in mouse colitis (information only; the literature review found two that were: RFTN1 and IKBIP. For the others a mouse model is the second experiment).</li></ul></div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>Groups instead of positions</h4>
<p class="small">Five of the genes could swap places depending on one weighting choice, so a single ranking would claim more than the data say. Each gene was scored six ways: the base points; without the protein light; without the UC literature light; without both; red lights costing 2 instead of 1; and an unmeasured protein counted as amber. <strong>Stability</strong> is the number of the six in which the gene is among the first ten (ties at the border included). <strong>Group A</strong>: in all six. <strong>Group B</strong>: in one to five, so its place depends on the weighting. <strong>Group C</strong>: in none. The rule was set before it was applied. Inside a group the order (stability, then points) carries no extra information.</p>
<p class="small"><strong>Protein bias.</strong> A gene whose protein was measured can earn up to two points that an unmeasured one cannot, and mass spectrometry quantifies abundant proteins more often: the {{ SL.summary.protein_bias.quantified }} genes quantified in the proteomics study sit at the {{ (SL.summary.protein_bias.median_rna_percentile_quantified * 100) | round | int }}th percentile of RNA abundance in inflamed mucosa, the rest at the {{ (SL.summary.protein_bias.median_rna_percentile_not_quantified * 100) | round | int }}th. Only {{ SL.summary.protein_quantified_in_shortlist }} of the {{ SL.rows | length }} candidates were measured. A grey protein light means not measured, not negative, and the "without protein" schemes show how much each gene leans on it.</p></div>
<div class="tw"><table class="sl"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Stable in top 10</th><th>Gene</th><th>Cell family</th><th class="num">Meta log2FC</th><th class="num">UC papers</th>
{% for k, label in SL.criteria %}<th>{{ label }}</th>{% endfor %}<th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in SL.rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.group }}</strong></td><td title="Position range across the six scorings: {{ r.best_rank }} to {{ r.worst_rank }}">{{ r.stability }} of 6</td><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.lineage }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.meta_log2fc }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.uc_papers }}</td>
{% for k, label in SL.criteria %}{% set v = r.criteria[k] %}<td class="c"><i class="lt {{ v[0] }}" title="{{ label }}: {{ v[1] }}" aria-label="{{ label }}: {{ v[0] }}, {{ v[1] }}"></i></td>{% endfor %}
<td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>Groups A and B, with the proposed first experiment</h3>
<p class="small">The experiment is chosen from the cell groups that hold most of the gene's RNA in inflamed single cells (a second one when it holds at least 20 %), not from the broad lineage: for example PCDH17 is a vessel gene (87 % in venules and endothelium), so its first test is endothelial cells, not fibroblasts. When a colitis study exists, its finding is added as the published lead.</p>
<div class="grid">
{% for r in SL.rows if r.group != "C" %}
<div class="card sl-card"><h3>Group {{ r.group }}: {{ r.gene }} <span class="small">{{ r.lineage }}, in the top 10 in {{ r.stability }} of 6 scorings</span></h3>
<dl>{% for k, label in SL.criteria %}{% set v = r.criteria[k] %}<dt><i class="lt {{ v[0] }}"></i> {{ label }}</dt><dd>{{ v[1] }}</dd>{% endfor %}
{% if r.atlases %}<dt>Four atlases</dt><dd>{% for a, v in r.atlases.items() %}{{ a }}: {% if v[0] %}{{ v[0] }} {{ (v[1] * 100) | round | int }} %{% else %}not measured{% endif %}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}</dd>{% endif %}</dl>
{% if r.papers %}<p class="small"><strong>Key papers:</strong></p><ul class="small">{% for p in r.papers %}<li>{% if p.pmid %}<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/{{ p.pmid }}/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID {{ p.pmid }}</a>{% else %}{{ p.pmcid }}{% endif %}{% if p.year %} ({{ p.year }}){% endif %}, {{ p.kind | replace('_', ' ') }}{% if p.direction in ('pathogenic', 'protective') %}, {{ p.direction }}{% endif %}: {{ p.summary }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>{% endif %}
<p class="small"><strong>Proposed:</strong> {{ r.experiment }}</p></div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
<h3 id="shortlist-literature">What the papers say, gene by gene</h3>
<p>For each candidate, the papers in a colitis or IBD setting that report something specific about the gene, with a one-line summary written by the classifier and checked against a sentence of the paper. Papers that only name the gene in a list of many genes or in a review are counted but not listed. Functional results in other diseases are shown in grey as context; they do not set the light.</p>
<ul class="small">
<li><strong>Expression</strong> (higher or lower in the disease) and <strong>biomarker</strong> results say the gene goes with the disease, not whether it causes it.</li>
<li><strong>Genetic</strong> results say the gene's region is linked to IBD risk, usually without a direction.</li>
<li>Only a <strong>functional</strong> study (the gene switched off, blocked or overexpressed) can say whether blocking it should help (green) or harm (red). Yellow means there is a specific result, but none of this kind.</li></ul>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Gene</th><th>Light</th><th>What the papers show</th><th class="num">Specific / list-only papers</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for L in SL.lit %}<tr><td>{{ L.group }}</td><td><strong>{{ L.gene }}</strong></td><td class="c"><i class="lt {{ L.light }}" aria-label="{{ L.light }}"></i></td>
<td>{% if L.findings %}<ul class="small" style="margin:0;padding-left:1.1em">{% for p in L.findings %}<li{% if not p.ibd_setting %} style="color:var(--muted)"{% endif %}>{% if p.pmid %}<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/{{ p.pmid }}/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID {{ p.pmid }}</a>{% else %}{{ p.pmcid }}{% endif %}{% if p.year %} ({{ p.year }}){% endif %}, {{ p.kind | replace('_', ' ') }}{% if p.direction in ('pathogenic', 'protective') %}, <strong>{{ p.direction }}</strong>{% endif %}{% if not p.ibd_setting %} (outside IBD){% endif %}: {{ p.summary }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>{% else %}<span class="small">{{ L.verdict }}</span>{% endif %}</td>
<td class="num">{{ L.about - L.lists }} / {{ L.lists }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>

<h3>Calibration: the same lights on known UC drug targets</h3>
<p>The {{ SL.cal | length }} UC clinical targets that are in the same gene table, scored with the same rules. Share of green lights:</p>
<div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th class="num">First-in-class genes</th><th class="num">Clinical targets</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for k, label in SL.criteria[:-1] %}<tr><td>{{ label }}</td><td class="num">{{ (SL.summary.green_share_first_in_class[k] * 100) | round | int }} %</td><td class="num">{{ (SL.summary.green_share_clinical_targets[k] * 100) | round | int }} %</td></tr>{% endfor %}
<tr><td><strong>Median points</strong></td><td class="num">{{ SL.summary.median_points_first_in_class }}</td><td class="num">{{ SL.summary.median_points_clinical_targets }}</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Clinical targets: {% for r in SL.cal %}{{ r.gene }} {{ r.score }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.</p>
<div class="howbox"><h4>What the calibration says</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Druggable</strong> passes every clinical target, as it should, and {{ (SL.summary.green_share_first_in_class.druggable * 100) | round | int }} % of the new genes.</li>
<li><strong>Safety</strong> is green for only {{ (SL.summary.green_share_clinical_targets.safety * 100) | round | int }} % of the clinical targets against {{ (SL.summary.green_share_first_in_class.safety * 100) | round | int }} % of the new genes. That is not because the new genes are safer: side effects and Mendelian diseases are known for genes that have been studied and drugged. A green safety light on a new gene means nothing is known, which is weaker than "safe". Many disease flags of the clinical targets are immunodeficiencies, the intended effect of an immunosuppressant.</li>
<li><strong>Tissue change</strong> is green for {{ (SL.summary.green_share_clinical_targets.tissue * 100) | round | int }} % of the clinical targets: several rise because immune cells arrive (IL23A, ICAM1), which is how an anti-inflammatory works. The first-in-class screen looks for a different kind of target, tissue that changes, so this light describes the kind of target rather than its odds of working.</li>
<li>No gene of either set has a green causal-genetics light: the genetic test is not where the targets of UC came from.</li></ul></div>
<div class="note"><strong>How to read this.</strong> No new gene meets every criterion: causal genetics is missing for almost all of them, because few genes have a genetic instrument, and only two have been tested in a mouse colitis model (RFTN1, IKBIP). In the colitis literature, two thirds of them are named only in gene lists or not at all: what supports them is this atlas. These are hypotheses ranked by data, to be tested in human cells first. The experiments are suggestions that follow from the cell source and the drug route; they are not designed protocols.</div>
</section>
